# Building with agents — speed with accuracy (all projects)

Agreed with Apurva on 2026-11-09 after the doctor-portal hand-off kit and design-system audit (doctor portal D-99, D-100).
Applies to every project: prototypes, PRDs, docs, analyses. Project files add their own commands; they don't relax this.

## Why this exists

Long check loops still missed a basic gap (design tokens for sizes, borders, timing, layering). Three causes:
- **Reviews checked work against its brief.** When the rule itself is wrong, the work passes every time.
- **The checking was upside down.** Slow checks (picture re-captures, AI critiques, full AI builds) ran on every change;
  fast automatic checks didn't exist.
- **Reviewers asked to find problems always find something.** Fixing every finding causes loops and bloat.

## The rules

1. **Size the job first.**
   - *Small* (a few files, mechanical: a wording key, a token, a rule line): the main thread does it directly, runs the one
     check that covers it, commits. No brief, no terminal, no AI review.
   - *Big* (build a journey, a new document, a migration): a terminal run from a brief, under the rules below.
2. **Foundation check before building on anything shared** (design system, kit, template, data model): 15 minutes on
   *"who uses this next, and what will they need?"*. Write the answer into the definition of done.
3. **Rules with no exceptions are tools, not instructions.** A linter, a test, a commit gate, a Claude Code hook. A line in
   CLAUDE.md is a guideline an agent may decide doesn't apply; a tool runs every time. Keep the tool set small.
4. **Fast checks always, slow checks once.**
   - Every edit / commit: linters and quick scripts (seconds).
   - At the start of a build: tests written from the spec by a different agent (Codex), before the build.
   - At the end of a build, once: the full check suite, picture compare if visuals changed, **one** review.
5. **One review, against the spec, correctness only.** The reviewer gets the spec and reports gaps that affect correctness
   (behaviour, rules, copy, tokens), skipping style and preferences. Use a different model family for logic and risky
   changes. All findings go on one list → **one fix pass**. A second review only if the first found blockers.
6. **Human eyes for looks.** Machines check rules and logic; Apurva's walkthrough on the phone link (about 10 minutes per
   journey) checks how it looks and feels. His notes join the same one list.
7. **Every bug gets its smallest test** (or lint rule) so it can't come back, plus a line in the project's feedback log.
8. **Time-box every run; half-page reports.** A run states its time limit; at the limit it reports what's left instead of
   looping. Screenshots only when someone's eyes are needed.

## Unattended runs

Before a run goes unattended, answer five questions (Anthropic's checklist): what "done" looks like; how it checks its
own work; what it may touch; what stops it (turn cap, time-box); who signs off.
- One fresh session per brief, chained by a runner script on the Mac (e.g. doctor portal `scripts/run-chain.sh`):
  queue → run → `DONE` / `STOPPED` line in `docs/runs/STATUS.md` → next. A `HOLD` file pauses it.
- The main thread reviews each report (scheduled check-in) and writes rulings to `docs/runs/RULINGS.md`, which every run
  reads first. Decisions are still recorded only by the main thread.
- Commits through `scripts/publish.sh` only; never `main`; no deploy unless Apurva asks.

## Sources

Anthropic, *Best practices for Claude Code* (hooks for no-exception rules; give the agent a pass/fail check; review
against the spec, skip style; the five questions before unattended runs) · Paweł Huryn (review results, not code;
smallest test per bug; a second model family for risky changes; human eyes for taste regressions) · Gabor Meyer on
Aakash Gupta's podcast (most effort in the spec; reviews before building) · the test pyramid (many fast checks, few
slow ones).
# tm-chotu local integration

`knowledge/context/tm-chotu/` contains the TrueMeds `tm-chotu` **v0.4.3** package (released 2026-08-25 by Truemeds Analytics, `gitlab.com/tm-exp/tm-chotu`; copied here 2026-10-16 from the ZIP Apurva supplied, replacing v0.1.20 of 2026-08-13). The package's own `.gitignore` is left out on purpose — it ignores `SKILL_REQUESTS/*.md`, and here gap notes must be committed. It is reusable reference context for Claude, Codex, and other tools working from this repository.

## Read order

1. Read `knowledge/context/tm-chotu/skills/using-tm-chotu/SKILL.md`.
2. Read only domain skills relevant to the task.
3. `knowledge/context/tm-chotu/KNOWLEDGE_DUMP.md` is a historical source since v0.4.2 — **the skills win on any conflict**.
4. For SQL or metrics, read `tm-chotu-query-rigor` and verify freshness/source.

## Truth handling

- Separate sourced facts, assumptions, derived values, and recommendations.
- Mark uncertain items `[UNVERIFIED]`; mark user-provided items `[USER-PROVIDED]`; mark inferences `[INFERRED]`.
- Do not invent metrics, schema, permissions, workflows, or operational policy.
- Live data still requires authorized connectors or user-provided exports.
- Do not store credentials, customer PII, or raw production extracts here.

## Tool switching

Open `/Users/mac/src/pm-agent` as project root in Claude/Codex. These root files govern agent behavior:

- `AGENTS.md` — shared agent protocol, **including the mandatory tm-chotu routing table** (Truemeds trigger → which skill file to read)
- `CLAUDE.md` — Claude-specific workspace guidance
- `context/Claude.md` — short global context
- this file — tm-chotu adapter and read order

For ChatGPT, add this repository to a Project or attach this adapter plus the relevant files under `knowledge/context/tm-chotu/`. Local files do not become global model memory automatically.

### Codex specifically — what you get vs. what you don't

Codex reads `AGENTS.md` automatically at session start; that's the *only* automatic
part. Everything past that is Codex reading plain markdown files as instructions —
there is no equivalent of the Claude Code plugin's mechanics:

| Claude Code plugin (if installed) | Codex (this repo, no install) |
|---|---|
| `SessionStart` hook auto-sets caveman mode | Not automatic — ask explicitly if wanted |
| Skills auto-route by description match | Codex must be told to read `AGENTS.md`'s routing table; it won't infer it from a skill description the way Claude Code's Skill tool does |
| `/tm-chotu-onboard`, `/tm-chotu-ask` etc. as slash commands | Don't exist in Codex — the command `.md` files under `knowledge/context/tm-chotu/commands/` are readable as reference text only, not invokable |
| Metabase/Mixpanel MCP auto-wired via plugin's `.mcp.json` | Codex uses its own `.codex/config.toml` MCP wiring (separate, already configured for other tools in this repo) — tm-chotu's `.mcp.json` is not read by Codex |
| Query-rigor / caveman / gap-loop enforced by hook | Enforced only if Codex actually follows `AGENTS.md`'s "Hard rules" — no runtime enforcement, so restate the rule if a session seems to drift |

Net effect: Codex gets the same underlying knowledge (definitions, table names,
join recipes, algo logic) but none of the automation. Treat `AGENTS.md`'s routing
table as the substitute for what the plugin's hook would otherwise do for you.

## Updates

Current: v0.4.3 (2026-08-25). New since v0.1.20: `tm-chotu-rx-cadence` (Rx / doctor-portal lifecycle), `tm-chotu-pii-register` (where personal data sits; rules), `tm-chotu-learnings` (failure ledger), `tm-chotu-excel-deliverable` (house .xlsx standard), `tm-chotu-mood-router` (Claude Code persona; not used here).


Replace only `knowledge/context/tm-chotu/` when a newer ZIP is approved. Review its `CHANGELOG.md` and `UPDATE.md` first. Keep this adapter and root instructions. Do not modify or push automatically.
